  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nWe propose an optimized initialization sequence that more effectively minimizes the maximum convolution value while maintaining normalization. The core idea is to use the relationship between the Gaussian-like distribution and the target lower bound 1.5027. By starting with a sequence that has higher peaks at the edges and a minimum sum of 1.5027, we can observe how the convolution behaves differently than usual applications.\n\nOur sequence will be generated by:\n1. Creating a base sequence that is a mixture of uniform and Gaussian distribution\n2. Applying a normalization factor of \\(\\sqrt{2}/4\\) to ensure the sum meets the requirement\n3. Generating the target Gaussian coefficients specifically to meet the convolution goal\n\nThis approach directly addresses the scale-invariance property of the optimization problem and reduces the likelihood of getting stuck in local minima by starting from a more \"fit\" initial distribution.\n\nMathematical specification:\n- We generate a sequence \\(a\\) of length \\(n\\) with non-negative elements (all coefficients between 0 and 1000)\n- The sequence must sum to at least 0.01 (normalized to 1)\n- The objective evaluated is \\(2n \\max(f*f)(i) / \\sum(a)^2\\)\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Create a base sequence of length \\(n\\) (initial candidates from 128, then 256)\n2. Use a Gaussian generator that takes advantage of the product properties to reflect the optimization goal\n3. Normalize the candidate sequence \\(a\\) such that \\(\\sum(a) = \\text{scaling factor}\\) for the Gaussian component\n4. Optimize the sequence using heuristic-based LP steps with less aggressive LP approximation and more gradient-descent-refinements\n5. Apply 10 full rounds of alternating optimization (LP solve and gradient descent)\n6. Always maintain the simplex projection (sum=1) and check for feasible solutions\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- Used only length 128 to reduce computation time sufficiently within 1800s limit\n- Focused on only one type of initialization (log-scaled Gaussian) to avoid search overhead\n- Applied dynamic LP step size with adaptive momentum and learning rate\n\nComputational budget:\nStatement probsmoothing with explicit optimization steps. The candidate generation remains efficient.\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- The final candidate must be a non-empty list of non-negative floats\n- Sequence normalization: sum >= 0.01 (now normalized to 1, which is >= 0.01)\n- Precise checker:\n\n  final_obj = float(evaluate_sequence(final_candidate))\n\n  if final_obj < target 1.5027:\n      # Optimization successful\n  else:\n      fallback to the last best candidate\n\nBudget estimation: \nWith 128-length sequences and one initialization type, this plan will run well within 1800 seconds.",
